Files in this distribution that derive from Schlep, with the proportion of
their executable lines that match the original verbatim:

    schlep-name.scm    52%
    schlep-base.scm    49%

The C back end of the original project, which was more closely derived from
Schlep, is not part of this distribution; it is not reached from the command
line entry point and was not carried over.

The type-annotation file format (scm2c.typ) is also inherited from Schlep and
is used unchanged.

SLIB (Aubrey Jaffer)
--------------------

Original: https://people.csail.mit.edu/jaffer/SLIB
slib-plt.scm provides SLIB compatibility bindings (slib:error, slib:eval,
defmacro, macroexpand) for Racket. The same copyright notice and conditions as
above apply.

On Lisp (Paul Graham)
---------------------

onlisp.scm is a Scheme/Racket rendering of utilities published in
Paul Graham, "On Lisp", Prentice Hall, 1993. The book's code is made freely
available by the author at http://www.paulgraham.com/onlisp.html

cKanren
-------

Used as an external dependency by the original relational type inference
(type-infer-ck.scm, type-infer-match.scm). Not redistributed with this project;
supply it through PLTCOLLECTS. https://github.com/calvis/cKanren

Boost
-----

The generated C++ and the runtime header scm2cpp.hpp use Boost, which is
distributed under the Boost Software License 1.0. Not redistributed here.

Benchmarks
----------

The FFT benchmark originates in the Gabriel benchmark suite (Harry Barrow,
1985; subsequently modified by Bob Shaw, Will Clinger and Jeffrey Mark
Siskind). The SICP examples follow Abelson and Sussman, "Structure and
Interpretation of Computer Programs".

